{
  "paragraphs": [
    "The ATOMIC phase III trial was a double‑blind, placebo‑controlled, parallel‑group study that randomised 185 adults with type 2 diabetes (HbA1c > 7.0 % < 10.0 %) and dyslipidaemia (LDL‑C > 100 < 250 mg/dL) to one of three arms: a combination of metformin + atorvastatin, metformin + atorvastatin placebo (metformin alone), or atorvastatin + metformin placebo (atorvastatin alone)【PubMed:38763510】.",
    "After 16 weeks, the combination arm showed a mean HbA1c change of +0.35 % from baseline, whereas the atorvastatin‑only arm changed by ‑0.58 % (a between‑group difference of 0.94 %, P < 0.0001). The proportion of participants whose HbA1c increased was 62 % in the combination group versus 15 % in the atorvastatin group (P < 0.001). For LDL‑C, the combination reduced levels by ‑55.20 % compared with a ‑7.69 % change in the metformin‑only arm (P < 0.001)【PubMed:38763510】.",
    "The abstract’s narrative conclusion states that adding atorvastatin to metformin “improved HbA1c and LDL‑C levels to a significant extent compared to metformin or atorvastatin alone” and suggests a preventive effect of metformin on the glucose‑elevating potential of atorvastatin. This narrative conflicts with the reported HbA1c numbers, which show the combination group’s HbA1c increased ( +0.35 %) while the atorvastatin‑only group’s HbA1c decreased ( ‑0.58 %). Both statements are presented as reported in the abstract without reconciliation【PubMed:38763510】.",
    "Older review articles describe metformin’s mechanisms (AMPK activation, reduced hepatic glucose output) and its broader benefits on lipids, inflammation, and cardiovascular risk, but they do not provide the specific 16‑week numerical outcomes reported in ATOMIC【PubMed:28770321】."
  ],
  "cited_sources": [
    "PubMed:38763510",
    "PubMed:28770321"
  ],
  "uncertainty":  [ "The data are limited to the abstract; full trial details, confidence intervals, and longer‑term outcomes are not available." ]
}

